INT. SLEEPER TRAIN - DINING CAR - NIGHT

White tablecloths, brass lamps swaying. The train rocks through a moonless forest. A single table is laid for six, but only five place settings are occupied.

LOUISE ARDEN (40s, pearls, composed) stirs her tea without drinking it. Her husband ARTHUR (50s, crisp suit) cuts a steak into perfect squares. DR. PELL (60s, round glasses, doctor's bag at his feet) reads the same page of a newspaper again and again. Their daughter MINNIE (7, ribbon in hair) draws on a napkin. A WAITER (20s, white jacket, stiff) hovers by the pantry door.

The sixth chair is empty. The sixth place setting is untouched.

ARTHUR
Another pot of tea, I think.

WAITER
Yes, sir.

The Waiter doesn't move. His eyes are on the empty chair.

LOUISE
The tea, please.

The Waiter jerks into motion. He vanishes through the pantry door. Dr. Pell turns a page that he hasn't read.

MINNIE
Mama, who is that man?

Louise sets down her spoon very gently.

LOUISE
What man, darling?

MINNIE
The one at the end. He's got no shoes.

Arthur's knife stops. Dr. Pell folds his newspaper into precise quarters.

ARTHUR
Minnie, eat your potatoes.

At the far end of the car, the lamp over the sixth chair dims. A PASSENGER (age impossible to tell, grey coat, wet hair, bare feet) is sitting there. No one saw him arrive.

DR. PELL
(not looking up)
Good evening.

PASSENGER
Evening, Doctor.

His voice is a dry rasp, like paper on stone. He rests both hands on the tablecloth. They leave damp prints.

ARTHUR
I don't believe we've been introduced.

PASSENGER
I don't believe you'd want to be.

Louise rises, smoothing her skirt, and walks to the sideboard. She lifts a heavy silver coffee pot and keeps her back to the table.

LOUISE
You're traveling a long way?

PASSENGER
Not as far as you.

Minnie slides off her chair and walks toward him with her napkin drawing. Arthur catches her wrist.

ARTHUR
Minnie.

MINNIE
He asked me to.

The Passenger takes the napkin. On it, in crayon, a stick figure lies under a stick train.

DR. PELL
(low)
Sexton. Enough.

The Passenger smiles. The dim lamp over him steadies, flaring warm.

SEXTON
So you do remember me, Doctor.

Dr. Pell stands, knocking his chair back. Louise sets down the coffee pot with a hard click. The Waiter comes through the pantry door with the tea tray and stops dead.

SEXTON (CONT'D)
Sit down, everyone. The night is long, and the train does not stop until we've settled the account.

Nobody sits. Nobody leaves. The train rocks on.
